  "articleBody": "It's easy to get excited about the potential of a foreclosed kitchen, but some common mistakes can quickly eat into your budget and time. One of the biggest pitfalls is underestimating hidden damage. You might see a dated kitchen, but what's behind those cabinets or under that old flooring? We've seen it all, from water damage to electrical issues that weren't obvious at first glance. Another mistake? Skipping contingency reserves. Distressed properties often have unexpected problems, so having extra funds set aside for those surprises is crucial. Finally, don't jump into cosmetic upgrades before you've confirmed that all the plumbing, electrical, and moisture problems are completely fixed. It's like putting a band-aid on a broken bone! To avoid these headaches, always get a detailed inspection done. Look for a contractor who provides a clear scope of work, includes photos of existing conditions, and gives you a transparent cost breakdown. This upfront planning can save you a lot of grief down the road. If you're seeing signs of major issues, like active leaks or structural concerns, that's when a qualified professional becomes super important. We can help you identify and tackle those bigger problems efficiently.",
